{"id": "npsaguaro", "name": "Saguaro National Park", "short": "Saguaro", "place": "Arizona, USA", "lat": 32.22, "lon": -110.9, "side": "l", "cont": "North America", "country": "United States of America", "states": ["Arizona"], "kind": "Desert", "park": true, "blurb": "Giant cacti, desert doves and the rains that keep the Sonoran Desert alive.", "tip": {"id": "rain", "title": "Dry out the desert", "text": "Water is a main driver of desert life. Set Winter and monsoon rain to -80% and watch the saguaro and the trees, then the animals that eat them."}, "sources": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Saguaro National Park", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Saguaro", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro"}, {"title": "National Park Service: Saguaro nature", "url": "https://www.nps.gov/sagu/learn/nature/index.htm"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Sonoran Desert", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonoran_Desert"}], "notes": "The story and why it matters come from the Wikipedia articles listed below (Saguaro National Park, Saguaro, Sonoran Desert). Individual species and links have not been checked one by one. Feeding links that match a GloBI (Global Biotic Interactions) record are marked in the node details, with a link to the records. A few links were added from GloBI records.", "imgwiki": "Saguaro National Park", "icon": "sage", "tier": "overview", "nodes": [{"id": "sun", "sp": "sunlight", "xh": 0.12, "desc": "Strong desert sun drives growth, but it also bakes the ground; shade under trees keeps young cacti alive."}, {"id": "rain", "sp": "winter and monsoon rain", "xh": 0.4}, {"id": "soil", "sp": "desert soil", "xh": 0.66, "desc": "Thin, dry soil whose nutrients return mainly through decay and the shade and litter under trees such as palo verde."}, {"id": "decomp", "sp": "soil fungi and bacteria", "xh": 0.88}, {"id": "saguaro", "sp": "carnegiea gigantea", "xh": 0.1, "wiki": "Saguaro", "ic": "sage", "badge": "Foundation species"}, {"id": "paloverde", "sp": "parkinsonia microphylla", "xh": 0.35}, {"id": "mesquite", "sp": "prosopis velutina", "xh": 0.6}, {"id": "pricklypear", "sp": "opuntia engelmannii", "xh": 0.85}, {"id": "dove", "sp": "zenaida asiatica", "xh": 0.04, "desc": "Eats saguaro fruit and seeds, and also pollinates saguaro flowers. Wikipedia says saguaro can make up most of its diet in the season."}, {"id": "woodpecker", "sp": "melanerpes uropygialis", "xh": 0.17, "tl": 2}, {"id": "bee", "sp": "apis mellifera", "xh": 0.3, "wiki": "Western honey bee", "desc": "Wikipedia lists honey bees among the main pollinators of saguaro, visiting its flowers for nectar.", "ic": "hopper"}, {"id": "jackrabbit", "sp": "lepus californicus", "xh": 0.43, "desc": "A large hare that grazes grasses and browses shrubs and cacti; prey for many desert predators."}, {"id": "peccary", "sp": "pecari tajacu", "xh": 0.57, "desc": "Also called javelina. Eats prickly pear pads and mesquite pods and travels in herds."}, {"id": "kratrat", "sp": "dipodomys merriami", "xh": 0.7, "desc": "A small seed-eating rodent of desert flats that does not need to drink; a main prey for snakes and owls."}, {"id": "muledeer", "sp": "odocoileus hemionus", "xh": 0.85, "wiki": "Mule deer", "desc": "The park's larger browser, taking leaves and twigs of desert shrubs and trees."}, {"id": "rattler", "sp": "crotalus atrox", "xh": 0.12, "desc": "An ambush predator of rodents, rabbits and birds."}, {"id": "gila", "sp": "heloderma suspectum", "xh": 0.37}, {"id": "grayfox", "sp": "urocyon cinereoargenteus", "xh": 0.62, "desc": "An omnivorous canid that hunts rodents and rabbits and eats fruit."}, {"id": "raven", "sp": "corvus corax", "xh": 0.87, "desc": "A scavenger that feeds on carrion such as animals killed on park roads."}, {"id": "coyote", "sp": "canis latrans", "xh": 0.2, "wiki": "Coyote", "desc": "A widespread predator and scavenger, taking rabbits, rodents, deer and carrion."}, {"id": "bobcat", "sp": "lynx rufus", "xh": 0.5, "wiki": "